      "wallWearComment": [
        "The top and bottom rims of a D-PLATEAU wall, worn. Same three terms as",
        "every other weathered boundary (see environment.weathering): the rim's",
        "reach is displaced, its outer edge dissolved, and soil painted over it.",
        "  reachWorldUnits - how far the wear spreads from a rim, measured as",
        "    GEODESIC distance over the terrain mesh, not through the air. World",
        "    units on purpose: a short terrace and a tall cliff wear by the same",
        "    physical amount, not by the same fraction of themselves, so where a",
        "    terrace is short enough the two bands MERGE - which is correct, a",
        "    short step reads as worn through.",
        "  min/maxAngleDegrees - the dihedral angle at the rim, between the",
        "    wall-side surface and the ground-side one, that the intensity ramps",
        "    over. THIS IS THE SAFETY PROPERTY, not a nicety: walls are often",
        "    flattened flush into the terrain on purpose, and wearing those rims",
        "    would draw a step the map author deliberately erased. At zero angle",
        "    the intensity is zero and the whole treatment switches itself off.",
        "    Measured from the geometry, never read off the D-PLATEAU or PLATEAU",
        "    WALL SLOPE settings, which is what lets a partially flattened wall",
        "    fade smoothly along its own length instead of snapping.",
        "  top / bottom - one substance, two exposures. A top rim spalls and",
        "    sheds, so it is drier, paler, tighter to the fold and more broken",
        "    up; a bottom rim collects what fell, so it is damper, darker and",
        "    spread wider. Two substances here would be two materials pretending",
        "    to be the same weather on two ends of one wall.",
        "  matte - how much of the ore reflection the wear suppresses where the",
        "    two overlap. Dirt is not metal, and ore regularly runs off its flat",
        "    pad and down a terrace."
      ],
